    "**Questions to answer:**\n",
    "(a) What differences did you observe between the unscaled and scaled models (accuracy, warnings, training time)?\n",
    "\n",
    "*Your answer:* The unscaled model performed extremely poorly (Accuracy = 0.3880, F1 = 0.2526) compared to the scaled model (Accuracy = 0.8067, F1 = 0.5985). The unscaled SVM hit the max_iter limit (convergence warning) and still produced a near-random classifier. The scaled model converged properly. Interestingly, the scaled model took slightly longer (3.85s vs 0.83s) because the unscaled model stopped early due to the iteration limit rather than actually converging.\n",
    "\n",
    "(b) Why does an unscaled linear SVM behave this way, in terms of how the margin/decision boundary is computed?\n",
    "\n",
    "*Your answer:* SVM computes the margin as the distance between the decision hyperplane and the nearest support vectors. When features are on vastly different scales (e.g. `TotalCharges` ranges 0–8,000 while binary features are 0/1), the distance calculation is dominated by the large-scale features. The optimizer struggles to find the correct hyperplane orientation because the gradients are poorly conditioned. The margin is effectively only sensitive to `TotalCharges` and `MonthlyCharges`, ignoring the smaller-scale but informative categorical features.\n",
    "\n",
    "(c) From this point onward in the lab, which version will you use for every subsequent SVM, and why?\n",
    "\n",
    "*Your answer:* We will use **scaled features** for every subsequent SVM. Scaling ensures all features contribute equally to the margin calculation and allows the SVM solver to converge properly. This is a mandatory preprocessing step for distance-based models like SVM.\n"
