    "**Questions to answer:**\n",
    "(a) How does the shape/smoothness of the decision boundary change as C increases?\n",
    "\n",
    "*Your answer:* As C increases, the decision boundary becomes **more complex and less smooth**. With C = 0.01, the boundary is very smooth and broad — almost a single smooth curve separating the two classes. At C = 1, the boundary starts showing more curvature to accommodate individual data points. At C = 100, the boundary becomes highly irregular and jagged, trying to correctly classify as many training points as possible, even those deep in the opposite class's region.\n",
    "\n",
    "(b) How does the number of support vectors change with C — does this match the theory?\n",
    "\n",
    "*Your answer:* The number of support vectors **decreases** as C increases: 2,978 (C=0.01) → 2,631 (C=1) → 2,603 (C=100). This matches the theory: a **small C** allows a wide margin with many violations (more support vectors), while a **large C** penalizes violations heavily, resulting in a narrow margin with fewer points on or inside it. Fewer data points fall within the tighter margin, so fewer support vectors are needed.\n",
    "\n",
    "(c) Which C looks like it is overfitting on this 2-D view, and which looks like it is underfitting? Explain the visual evidence.\n",
    "\n",
    "*Your answer:* **C = 100 appears to be overfitting** — the decision boundary is irregular and tightly wraps around individual points, suggesting it is memorizing training noise rather than learning the true pattern. **C = 0.01 appears to be underfitting** — the boundary is too smooth and simple, almost treating everything as one class in some regions, failing to capture the actual class separation. **C = 1** provides the best trade-off between smoothness and detail.\n",
    "\n",
    "(d) Why is this 2-D plot only an APPROXIMATE illustration of the real (high-dimensional) SVM? What could it be misleading you about?\n",
    "\n",
    "*Your answer:* The PCA projection retains only **45.03%** of the total variance (33.02% + 12.01%). This means more than half of the information in the original 30 features is lost. The 2-D plot could be misleading about: (1) the actual separability of the classes — they may be well-separated in higher dimensions but overlapping in 2-D; (2) the true shape of the decision boundary — a boundary that looks complex in 2-D might be simple in the full space (or vice versa); (3) which features actually drive the separation — the principal components are linear combinations of all features, obscuring feature importance.\n"
   ]
